00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:17.730
In August 2017, the Democratic People's Republic
of Korea, in it’s most provocative move

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.710
to date, tested a long range intercontinental
ballistic missiles, which flew directly over

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.779
the Japanese Northern Island of Hokkaido and
set sirens wailing.

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:31.070
This is just the latest in North Korean missile
test, with the previous month's test proving

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:34.930
the North Korean dictatorship had the capability
of reaching American soil.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:38.860
The engineers of North Korea have overcome
many obstacles to get to this point, but just

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.270
achieving the necessary range is, thankfully,
half the battle.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.340
Today we are going to investigate the history
behind these long range weapons and why they

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.410
are so difficult to engineer.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:55.420
An Intercontinental ballistic missile, or
ICBM, is a missile that follows a ballistic

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:56.420
trajectory.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.170
Quite simply, the path it follows is the same
as that of a thrown or propelled projectile

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.140
would take under the act of gravity.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.670
Think of it as a projectile that is thrown
extraordinarily fast and high by a relatively

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.830
brief, yet powerful, rocket engine.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.690
Like many weapons of mass destruction, the
Nazis were some of the first to theorize an

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:13.690
ICBM.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.830
“Projekt Amerika”, led by Wernher von
Braun, was a code name for a weapon being

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:22.810
developed that could be used against New York
and other American cities during WWII.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.820
Just as the V2, which was the world’s first
long-range ballistic missile, had been used

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.520
to wreak havoc on London.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.430
Luckily the war ended before it was fully
developed.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:36.909
but many of these same engineers were drafted
by the US and USSR after WWII to help design

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:41.030
their first rockets, and many of these design
philosophies laid the groundwork for what

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.619
we see today.

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:46.909
These early ICBM designs were essentially
upgraded versions of the V2 bombs.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:50.659
The V2 was redesigned to include wings and
dubbed the A9.

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:55.100
This would sit on top of the huge first stage,
dubbed the A10, which would boost the V2 from

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.639
it’s original range of 300 kilometres, which
was enough to reach London from the Netherlands,

00:01:59.639 --> 00:02:03.990
to 5000 km, enough to reach the American eastern
seaboard from Ireland.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.630
The German engineers aimed the V2 simply by
setting a compass heading before launch, which

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.810
allowed the internal guidance system consisting
of gyroscopes to keep the rocket on course

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.000
while the rockets were firing.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.720
Gyroscopes allow a missile to measure its
deviation from initial flight trajectories.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.860
Spinning masses, like a gyroscope, want to
maintain the direction their axis of rotation

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:23.860
is pointing.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:28.140
So if we set the axis of rotation in the direction
of desired travel, and mount the gyroscope

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.730
in a frame that does not transfer the missiles
rotation to the gyro, the missile can measure

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:36.240
that change in rotation and correct it’s
flight with control fins.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.290
The German’s controlled the range by simply
adjusting the amount of fuel in the rocket,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.650
using a slide rule to calculate the fuel needed
for a given trajectory.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.620
Although this was incredibly advanced technology
for the time, the V2s were still notoriously

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.830
inaccurate, because it relied completely on
initial calculations on the ground and had

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.680
no way of correcting for unexpected deviations.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.870
But London was a big target, and the Netherlands
weren’t that far away, and the Germans certainly

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.010
did not care who the bombs killed.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:06.640
To be effective against a target much further
away would require a finer tuned guidance

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.620
system, which the Germans did not have.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.090
So, incredibly, these early ICBMs were planned
to be manned.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:17.740
After blasting off from Europe, exiting earth’s
atmosphere, separating the first stage, reaching

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.980
a max speed 10 times greater than the speed
of sound, the second stage would come hurtling

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.600
back through into the atmosphere, where it
would glide to reach it’s final target.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.790
If they hadn’t already died from the heat
of re-entry the pilot would then set their

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:33.870
final trajectory using radio guidance from
surfaced German submarines and eject.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:38.070
Only to be promptly killed by the force of
impact of air hitting his head and chest.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.300
This was the German’s kamikaze strategy.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.000
In an era before onboard computers and GPS
existed, developing a guidance systems was

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.120
half the battle.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.940
Modern rockets use a combination of that gyroscope
based inertial guidance, satellite positioning

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.930
and terrain mapping, which uses altitude maps
of the route to the target to guide the missile.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:59.880
You may wonder why the Germans put so much
effort into creating space age weapons, only

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.190
to equip them with bombs with less power than
those they could drop from planes.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:06.320
Well this is arguably the hardest part of
the equation.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:10.460
The longer the range and the heavier the load,
the more complex the rocket.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:15.340
Just adding a first stage adds a significant
amount of complexity and cost to the design.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.639
Adding a massive bomb was simply not feasible
and that’s just an issue of size and weight.

00:04:19.639 --> 00:04:23.180
The German’s were limited with the type
of bomb they could use for another reason.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.990
This is a size comparison between the V2 rockets
and the two nuclear bombs dropped on Japan.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:32.350
Sizewise, it’s not inconceivable that these
bombs could have been integrated into the

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:38.289
V2, but Fat man and little boy weighed 4.6
tonnes and 4.4 tonnes respectively, so weight

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:39.560
would have been a huge issue.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.909
The V2’s warhead consisted of a 1 tonne
amatol bomb, which is a TNT based explosive.

00:04:44.909 --> 00:04:48.389
TNT’s primary advantage is its high activation
energy.

00:04:48.389 --> 00:04:52.550
Meaning it would not easily detonate from
a sudden impact shock or from heat.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:57.580
Even if small - lightweight nuclear bombs
existed at the time, neither America or Germany

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.349
had the technology required to launch them
with a missile, because of the intense heat

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:06.199
associated with re-entry of ICBMs would destroy
the warhead before it ever got close to the

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:07.639
target.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:12.199
Re-entry vehicles typically used a combination
of blunt body design, ablative materials,

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:14.509
heat sinks and insulating materials.

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:18.379
Blunt body design allows the re-entry vehicle
to create a bow shock wave in front of the

00:05:18.379 --> 00:05:22.951
vehicle that keeps the super heated plasma
a little further from vehicles surface, with

00:05:22.951 --> 00:05:25.400
an insulating boundary layer of air in between.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:30.129
Ablative materials, like the single use Gemini
heat shield,, burn or melt and then detach

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:34.999
from the vehicle carrying some of that super
heated plasma away and heat sinks use conductive

00:05:34.999 --> 00:05:39.131
and heat resistant materials like Beryllium
to spread some of that heat and allow it to

00:05:39.131 --> 00:05:40.669
be radiated away.

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:44.909
The Discovery Space Shuttle used reusable
and replaceable thermal tiles and blankets,

00:05:44.909 --> 00:05:48.569
while the nose cone was a carbon-carbon composite
which acted as a heat sink.

00:05:48.569 --> 00:05:50.889
But all of these things have one thing in
common.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:54.159
They add bulk and mass to the design once
again.

00:05:54.159 --> 00:05:58.620
Simply put, designing a rocket capable of
launching out earth's atmosphere is arguably

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:02.849
the easiest and most well documented part
of building an ICBM.

00:06:02.849 --> 00:06:06.979
Miniaturising nuclear bombs capable of withstanding
the journey and creating a guidance system

00:06:06.979 --> 00:06:10.580
accurate enough to hit the target is a whole
other challenge.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:14.389
We know North Korea is struggling with this
part of their missile program, as the re-entry

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:18.939
of their July launch was caught on CCTV, showing
the missile burning up and disintegrating

00:06:18.939 --> 00:06:23.069
before landing in the sea off the coast of
Japan and their recent test had a similar

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:24.069
fate.

00:06:24.069 --> 00:06:27.979
So even if North Korea have a miniaturized
Nuclear bomb, it’s unlikely it would survive

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:31.979
the journey and when it comes to guidance,
North Korth do not have their own positioning

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:36.129
satellites, and it’s unlikely that China
have granted them access to their high accuracy

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:37.999
military positioning system.

00:06:37.999 --> 00:06:41.680
For now North Korea don’t pose much of a
threat to America, but if they did somehow

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.719
develop an ICBM capable of carrying nuclear
weapons, despite the heavy embargoes on the

00:06:46.719 --> 00:06:51.370
country, the world has moved on since the
days of V2 bombardment of defenceless London.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:54.439
There are countermeasures and we will explore
them in the next video.

